  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 04d79210d2564f739f9a05a738b8a1d3, type: 3}
  m_Name: ReadMe
  m_EditorClassIdentifier:
  data:
    title: Event Listener Showcase
    summary: A tiny Neon Arcade with three objects picked for event variety - a manual
      Claw Machine, a timed Hype Boost, and an automation Technician - plus a pinned row
      of listener chips above the scrolling dashboard. One currency pays for everything,
      nothing auto-produces out of the box, and every chip flash follows a user action
      or a consequence of one. The feed line under the chips shows the real event member
      and its payload.
    resourcePrefix: demo_events
    sections:
    - heading: The progression arc
      body: 'Start and claim Claw Machine rounds to earn Credits, then buy more machines
        (the Add +100 button on the Credits card tops you up if you want to move faster).
        Buy and use the Hype Boost: Boosts Changed fires once immediately and again about
        20 seconds later when the boost expires by itself. Hire the Technician once for
        auto start and twice for auto collect - from then on the production and currency
        chips flash hands-free through the exact same listeners as manual play.'
    - heading: Why chips flash together sometimes
      body: One chip per distinct action, so simultaneous flashes are meaningful rather
        than duplicates - buying a machine lights Business Bought and Currency Changed
        together because the purchase costs money. Granular twin members (holder-level
        variants, amount-added-with-source variants) exist for most of these events; the
        scripting reference lists them per manager.
    - heading: How to hook up your own UI
      body: Each chip is one AddListener call on a public UnityEvent, registered once and
        removed in OnDestroy. Copy the same pattern for popups, screen shake, or sounds -
        subscribe to the manager event, read the payload, and drive your feedback from it
        instead of polling state every frame.
    - heading: Other systems work the same way
      body: Upgrades, achievements, shop items, offers, daily rewards, locations, player
        stats, and prestige all expose the same style of UnityEvent families on their
        managers (OnUpgradeApplied, OnAchievementClaimed, OnOfferBecameAvailable,
        OnDailyRewardClaimed, OnPrestige, and more). Add the object to your game and
        subscribe exactly like this demo does.
